   Martinez-Villar, Jose Manuel},
Title = {María Ángeles Valdemoros San Emeterio. . valdemoros@unirioja.es},
Journal = {PEDAGOGIA SOCIAL REVISTA INTERUNIVERSITARIA},
Year = {2024},
Number = {44},
Pages = {133-146},
Month = {JAN-JUN},
Abstract = {Intergenerational environmental leisure and, more specifically, that
   shared between grandfathers and grandmothers with their grandsons and
   granddaughters, provides numerous benefits. As it is a dimension of
   leisure directly linked to nature and outdoor activities, it contributes
   to the achievement of different objectives compatible with sustainable
   development, being perceived as an enriching, attractive and flexible
   experience, which coheres and strengthens the ties between generations.
   The purpose of this work was to examine shared environmental leisure
   between grandparents1 and grandchildren, and the motivations linked to
   said practice. Quantitative (N=350) and qualitative (N=18) methodology
   was combined, using an ad hoc questionnaire and a discussion group,
   respectively. The results show that intergenerational leisure activities
   in natural environments are increasingly practiced; Travel, as well as
   caring for plants, gardens and animals are the preferred activities. The
   preferred reasons for carrying out these shared experiences are the
   transmission of knowledge and affection, and the care that grandparents
   profess to their grandchildren. The prospective aims to deepen whether
   the value given to environmental leisure has grown after the pandemic,
   due to its potential to facilitate intergenerational contact in safe
   environments.},
