   Fallah and Feng, Xiaoqi and Fernandes, Amanda and Fuertes, Elaine and
   Giannico, Vincenzo and Gouveia, Nelson and Hartig, Terry and Heinrich,
   Joachim and Hystad, Perry and Ibarluzea, Jesus and Jacquemin, Benedicte
   and James, Peter and Jashni, Mahsa and Knibbs, Luke D. and Knobel, Pablo
   and Kogevinas, Manolis and Lertxundi, Aitana and Markevych, Iana and
   Mehrparvar, Amirhooshang and Miri, Mohammad and Mitchell, Richard and
   Nawrot, Tim S. and Nieuwenhuijsen, Mark J. and O'Callaghan-Gordo,
   Cristina and Pearce, Jamie and Plusquin, Michelle and Sanesi, Giovanni
   and Su, Jason G. and Triguero-Mas, Margarita and Ubalde-Lopez, Monica
   and Valentin, Antonia and White, Mathew P. and Yang, Bo-Yi and Yang, Jun
   and Zhang, Jinguang and Zhao, Tianyu and Helbich, Marco and Dadvand,
   Payam},
Title = {Methodological guidance for selecting buffers in greenspace-health
   studies},
Journal = {LANCET PLANETARY HEALTH},
Year = {2025},
Volume = {9},
Number = {11},
Month = {NOV},
Abstract = {Greenspace can promote health via diverse pathways. A common approach to
   assessing greenspace exposure is to estimate vegetation availability
   within buffers surrounding locations where people reside or spend time.
   However, no clear framework for informed buffer selection exists, and
   choices made show considerable heterogeneity, impeding evidence
   synthesis and causal inference. In this Personal View conducted by an
   interdisciplinary panel of experts, we aimed to establish a framework
   for informed buffer selection for epidemiological studies on greenspace.
   We began by reviewing available approaches for the selection of buffer
   types, which range from single fixed-location approaches to
   high-resolution mobility-based activity-space approaches, as well as
   different buffer sizes. We then summarised the determinants of buffer
   type and size selection including health outcomes and underlying
   mechanisms, study population, contextual factors, and data
   characteristics. Finally, based on these determinants, we developed
   recommendations for future research. Buffer type and size selection
   should be hypothesis driven, reflecting presumed greenspace-health
   mechanisms. Buffer selection should target activity-based approaches
   where feasible, and multiple buffer sizes should be tested. Overall, the
   assessment of greenspace exposure should shift from ad-hoc approaches to
   personalised, multiscale, and context-specific methods. We call for
   standardising and reporting the rationale for buffer selection to
   minimise bias and enhance comparability and evidence synthesis across
   studies.},
